{"name":"Brilliant Blue","hex":"#0075b3","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"brilliant","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Shining brightly.","Both bright and saturated."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A finely cut gemstone, especially a diamond, cut in a particular form with numerous facets so as to maximize light return through the top (called \"table\") of the stone.","One of many hummingbird species of the genus Heliodoxa."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brilliant"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"List of RAL colours","blocks":[{"text":"The following is a list of RAL Classic colours from the European RAL colour standard. The visual samples are approximate and informative only."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_RAL_colours","wikidata":"Q17163521"},{"title":"Brilliant blue FCF","blocks":[{"text":"Brilliant blue FCF is a synthetic organic compound used primarily as a blue colorant for processed foods, medications, dietary supplements, and cosmetics. It is classified as a triarylmethane dye and is known under various names, such as FD&C Blue No. 1 or acid blue 9. It is denoted by E number E133 and has a color index of 42090. It has the appearance of a blue powder and is soluble in water and glycerol, with a maximum absorption at about 628 nanometers. It is one of the oldest FDA-approved color additives, having been permanently listed for use in food and ingested drugs in 1969. It is generally considered nontoxic and safe for consumption."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Brilliant_Blue_FCF_ball-and-stick.png/330px-Brilliant_Blue_FCF_ball-and-stick.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brilliant_blue_FCF","wikidata":"Q420093"},{"title":"Brilliant Blue (manga)","blocks":[{"text":"Brilliant Blue  is a Japanese manga series written and illustrated by Saemi Yorita. It is licensed in North America by Digital Manga Publishing, which released the first volume of the manga through its DokiDoki imprint, on 6 May 2009. The second volume was released on 19 August 2009. Yorita considers Nanami to be a \"stupid bottom\" character."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brilliant_Blue_(manga)","wikidata":"Q4968041"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Jolly_Ranchers_-_blue_and_red_%28cropped%29.png/500px-Jolly_Ranchers_-_blue_and_red_%28cropped%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Jolly_Ranchers_-_blue_and_red_%28cropped%29.png/500px-Jolly_Ranchers_-_blue_and_red_%28cropped%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Blue and red Jolly Rancher candy. The blue color is brilliant blue FCF dye.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJolly_Ranchers_-_blue_and_red_(cropped).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Vodka_and_Blue_Curacao_%28with_Brilliant_Blue_FCF%29.jpg/500px-Vodka_and_Blue_Curacao_%28with_Brilliant_Blue_FCF%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Vodka_and_Blue_Curacao_%28with_Brilliant_Blue_FCF%29.jpg/500px-Vodka_and_Blue_Curacao_%28with_Brilliant_Blue_FCF%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Vodka with Blue Curaçao syrup","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVodka_and_Blue_Curacao_(with_Brilliant_Blue_FCF).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Blue_smarties.JPG/500px-Blue_smarties.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Blue_smarties.JPG/1280px-Blue_smarties.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Blue_smarties.JPG/500px-Blue_smarties.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In the United Kingdom, Smarties chocolates were colored with brilliant blue FCF (top) until 2008, later being replaced with a natural spirulina coloring (bottom).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlue_smarties.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/2010._%D0%94%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%86%D0%BA._%D0%9A%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BB_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%8C_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D